<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SSML pronunciation lexicon (W3C PLS 1.0) for the en-US Voice Live voice.
  Wired in through the voice's custom_lexicon_url (services/ws/azure_session_config.py).

  Why aliases in Arabic script: the voice is a Dragon HD voice, and HD voices apply
  only <alias> entries from a lexicon; <phoneme> entries are ignored. HD voices are
  multilingual, so an Arabic-script alias makes the voice say that word in Arabic
  inside the English reply. The swap happens in TTS only: subtitles and transcripts
  keep the English spelling.

  Every alias carries full vowel marks (tashkeel). Unmarked Arabic is ambiguous and
  the voice guesses the vowels: حمام is حَمَام (pigeons) or حَمَّام (bathroom).

  Azure rules this file must follow:
  - Graphemes are CASE SENSITIVE: every spelling the bot or the Branches data can
    produce needs its own <grapheme> ("Wakrah", "Wakra", "WAKRAH").
  - A grapheme can be a phrase only with an <alias> ("Al Rayyan", "Umm Salal
    Mohammed"); a <phoneme> is per word. Single words carry no article
    ("Rayyan" -> رَيَّان) so text not matched as a phrase never says it twice.
  - Phonemes: en-US IPA phone set only, no spaces, "." between syllables.
  - Max 100 KB. Azure caches a lexicon per URL for 15 minutes; the URL carries
    the file's content version, so a changed file is fetched again at once.

  New branch or name: add a lexeme with its Title-case AND ALL-CAPS spelling and a
  vowel-marked Arabic alias; tests/test_tts_lexicon.py checks the rules above.
-->
